extras.note:
Front-door pattern. Define each model with a launch cmd pointing at native Windows llama-server.exe, e.g.:
models: "qwen3-14b": { cmd: 'llama-server.exe -m D:/AI-Models/qwen3-14b-q5_k_m.gguf -ngl 99 --port ${PORT} --flash-attn --cont-batching --parallel 4', ttl: 300 } "big-32b-offload": { cmd: 'llama-server.exe -m D:/AI-Models/qwen3-32b-q4_k_m.gguf -ngl 60 --port ${PORT} --flash-attn', ttl: 120 } "bge-rerank": { cmd: 'llama-server.exe -m D:/AI-Models/bge-reranker-v2-m3.gguf --reranking --port ${PORT}', ttl: 600 }
Key knobs: ttl unloads idle models to free the 32 GB VRAM; -ngl 99 = full GPU offload (drop it to spill big models into the 64 GB system RAM); groups: keep small models co-resident. Install via `winget install llama-swap` (native, no WSL2).

Pure-Python, runs native on Windows (`pip install 'litellm[proxy]'; litellm --config config.yaml`). Blend local + hosted:
model_list: - model_name: local-fast litellm_params: { model: openai/qwen3-14b, api_base: http://localhost:8080/v1, api_key: sk-noop, order: 1 } - model_name: local-fast # same alias = load-balance / fallback litellm_params: { model: anthropic/claude-..., order: 2 }
router_settings: { routing_strategy: latency-based-routing, num_retries: 2 }
Order=1 tried first; on failure escalates to order=2 (hosted) after its retries. Add general_settings for virtual keys + max_budget per key. Only add this layer if you need multi-provider routing/budgets — otherwise llama-swap already multiplexes local models.
